## The one change that fixes your error

`USER 1001` → `USER default`

That's the whole fix. `USER 1001` couldn't write to `/home/default/.fortify/` where Fortify stores its session, so every file failed with `IOException: No such file or directory`. Running as `default` (the base image's own user) restores write access.

## Build and push (tag 1.1)

```bash
unzip gradle-9.4.1-bin.zip          # if gradle-9.4.1/ not already extracted

podman build --build-arg GRADLE_VERSION=9.4.1 \
  -t artifactory.jfrog.sbi:443/dso-docker-base-images/epay-fortify-gradle:1.1 .

podman tag artifactory.jfrog.sbi:443/dso-docker-base-images/epay-fortify-gradle:1.1 \
           artifactory.jfrog.sbi:443/itepaypg-sbiepay2-docker-local/sast/epay-fortify-gradle:1.1

podman push artifactory.jfrog.sbi:443/itepaypg-sbiepay2-docker-local/sast/epay-fortify-gradle:1.1 --tls-verify=false
```

I used **tag `1.1`** deliberately — your runner's `if-not-present` pull policy would otherwise reuse the cached broken `1.0` layer. New tag forces a fresh pull. Remember to update the sast job image to `:1.1` to match.

## One thing to verify while building

If `podman build` fails at the `USER default` line with "user default not found," it means the base image uses a different user name. Quick check:
```bash
podman run --rm artifactory.jfrog.sbi:443/dso-base-image/ubi9/ubi9-java21-fortify-24.4:2.0 whoami
```
Whatever that prints is the user to put in the `USER` line. The error path in your log (`/home/default/.fortify`) strongly indicates it's `default`, but that one command confirms it for certain.
